I've completed the full analysis. Here is the formal report.

---

## testsmells.org 19-Smell Academic Audit

**Target:** `DataAccess.IntegrationTests/UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`
**Framework:** MSTest 4.1.0 · .NET 10 · C#
**Integration context:** `[TestCategory("Integration")]` — calibration applied per Spadini et al. (MSR 2020)

---

### 1. Summary Dashboard

```
┌──────────┬─────────────┬────────────────┐
│ Severity │ Smell Count │ Affected Tests │
├──────────┼─────────────┼────────────────┤
│ High     │ 2           │ 2              │
│ Medium   │ 2           │ 2              │
│ Low      │ 0           │ 0              │
│ Total    │ 4           │ 4              │
└──────────┴─────────────┴────────────────┘
```

---

### 2. Findings by Severity

#### ❶ Conditional Test Logic — HIGH

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` · **Method:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (line 96)

The `if`/`else` branch means one assertion path **never executes**. If `loaded` is always a base `User`, the `PremiumUser` branch is dead code giving false coverage.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — branching changes which assertion runs
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Fix — split into two deterministic tests:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_StandardUser_ReturnsCorrectName()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_PremiumUser_HasPositiveDiscount()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("eve@example.com", "Eve", discountRate: 0.1m));
    var loaded = (PremiumUser)_repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com")!;

    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Hidden testing gap — one branch is permanently untested, giving false confidence in type-dispatch logic.

---

#### ❷ Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test) — HIGH

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` · **Method:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (line 112)

This test inserts 100 users but **never asserts anything**. MSTest reports it as passing as long as no exception is thrown — even if every insert silently failed.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — no assertions at all
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
}
```

**Fix — assert the observable outcome:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    const int count = 100;
    for (int i = 0; i < count; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(count, users.Count);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False-pass — the test provides zero verification. Any silent data-loss bug is invisible.

---

#### ❸ Sleepy Test — MEDIUM *(downgraded from High; integration-calibrated)*

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` · **Method:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 82)

`Thread.Sleep(3000)` adds 3 seconds of dead time per run and is **non-deterministic** — on a slow CI agent the event may need more time; on a fast one the delay is pure waste.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — fixed delay, blocks the thread
Thread.Sleep(3000);
Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
```

**Fix — poll with a timeout:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    var sent = await WaitUntilAsync(
        () => _repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id),
        timeout: TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

    Assert.IsTrue(sent, "Notification was not sent within the timeout.");
}

private static async Task<bool> WaitUntilAsync(Func<bool> condition, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    var deadline = DateTime.UtcNow + timeout;
    while (DateTime.UtcNow < deadline)
    {
        if (condition()) return true;
        await Task.Delay(100);
    }
    return false;
}
```

**Calibration note:** Downgraded from High because integration tests sometimes genuinely coordinate with async infrastructure. Still flagged because a polling pattern is strictly superior.

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky failures on slow machines; 3 s of wasted wall-clock time per run that compounds across CI.

---

#### ❹ Assertion Roulette — MEDIUM

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` · **Method:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (line 28)

Four assertions with **no diagnostic messages**. When one fails, the MSTest output shows only the generic failure — the developer must read the source to identify which property was wrong.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — which of these 4 failed?
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

**Fix — add messages to each assertion:**

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert.");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should round-trip through persistence.");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should round-trip through persistence.");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Auto-generated Id should be positive.");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slower diagnosis during CI failures; developers must read source to interpret red builds.

---

### 3. Smell-Free Patterns ✅

The remaining **4 tests** are well-designed:

| Test | Notes |
|------|-------|
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Clear arrange/act/assert; 2 focused assertions |
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Single assertion on the expected null result |
| `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` | Count of 3 is contextually obvious (not Magic Number); `CollectionAssert` is idiomatic |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Good concurrency test; asserts the outcome; count of 10 matches the Range |

The `[TestInitialize]`/`[TestCleanup]` fixture is tight — both `_connection` and `_repository` are used by every test, so **General Fixture** does not apply.

---

### 4. Smells Ruled Out (15 of 19 — clean)

| Smell | Verdict |
|-------|---------|
| Mystery Guest | ✅ In-memory SQLite — no external resources (integration-calibrated) |
| Eager Test | ✅ No test calls 4+ distinct production methods (integration-calibrated) |
| Magic Number Test | ✅ All literals (3, 10, 100) are contextually self-documenting |
| Sensitive Equality | ✅ No `.ToString()` comparisons |
| Exception Handling | ✅ No `try`/`catch` blocks |
| General Fixture | ✅ Both fields used by all 8 tests |
| Ignored Test | ✅ No `[Ignore]` or skip attributes |
| Duplicate Assert | ✅ No repeated identical assertions |
| Lazy Test | ✅ Each test targets distinct behavior |
| Constructor Init | ✅ Uses `[TestInitialize]`, not a constructor |
| Default Test | ✅ Class is properly named |
| Redundant Print | ✅ No `Console.WriteLine` |
| Redundant Assertion | ✅ No tautological assertions |
| Resource Optimism | ✅ In-memory DB created per-test |
| Empty Test | ✅ No empty method bodies |

---

### 5. Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Fix | Effort |
|----------|-------|-----|--------|
| 1 | **Assertion-Free Test** | Add `Assert.AreEqual(count, ...)` to `BulkInsert` | ~2 min |
